  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nAn elongated slow moving cold front continues to sag southeast \nacross the Ohio Valley region this evening into the overnight before\nstalling across the Kentucky Bluegrass region. This stalled boundary\nwill provide the focus for multiple rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms near and south of the Ohio River this evening into the\novernight. \n\nGiven MUCAPE values around 1000 to maybe 15000 J/kg and pockets of\n0-500 bulk shear around 30 knots, a low threat remains in our \nsouthern counties for some stronger storms to produce isolated \ndamaging winds, primarily during the early evening hours. \n\nTropical moisture continues to be advected across the region,\nallowing PWATS to reach 250 to 300% of normals for this time of year.\nThis, combined with skinny MUCAPE and a west to east storm motion, \nwill allow for efficient rainfall rates and introduce a \nflooding/flash flooding threat for areas along and south of the Ohio \nRiver this evening. While most areas is this region will receive\naround 0.5 to 0.75 in of rain, cannot rule out some isolated pockets\nof 2-3 inches where storms move over repeatedly. \n\nKey Message 2)\n\nBy Thursday, another developing wave of low pressure to our west \nwill move east across the Central Plains and through our region. This\nfeature will transition our stalled cold front/ stationary boundary \nacross Kentucky into a warm front, and lift it back north across the \nregion and pulling a new cold front toward the region from our west.\nShowers and thunderstorms will develop ahead of this new front on\nThursday evening. \n\nThe bulk of the rainfall Thursday evening into Friday looks to\nprimarily impact east central IN and west central OH and areas north.\nThe tropical moisture remains in place and PWATs will continue to\nhang around 250-300% of normal. Most areas will receive a half an \ninch to 0.75 in, but CAMs paint some stripes of 1-2+ inches across \nportions of this region, introducing a flash flood threat Thursday\nevening/overnight. \n\nTurning our attention to severe... While SBCAPE looks more limited \n(perhaps some splotchy 500-1000 J/kg near the Tri-State), a handful \nof CAMs paint MUCAPE between 1500-2000 J/kg, with the highest totals \nagain near the Tri- State. Shear looks a bit more impressive on \nThursday compared to Wednesday, with deep layer shear values between \n30 to 50 knots, with the higher values across east central IN and \nwest central OH. \n\nAdditionally, with a surface low moving right across Indiana, this\ndoes introduce quite a bit of directional shear to the forecast. The\nSupercell Parameter lights up across east central IN into the Tri-\nState from various CAMs and the Nadocast paints a 3% bullseye to our\nwest (IL/IN border) but does scrape our eastern IN/ western OH \ncounties with a 1-2%. While a tornado threat is quite low, we cannot \nrule a brief spin up. \n\nWith all that being said... tricky forecast indeed. Given that the \nweather will be arriving as we head into the evening hours, a low \nlevel inversion setting up will help pinch off SBCAPE. Additionally,\nthe higher shear values will be slightly displaced from the more\nrobust MUCAPE (based on today's guidance). \n\nKey Message 3)\nThe low pressure system will move through the Ohio Valley off to the\nnortheast and continue it's intensification, resulting in a strong\ncold front and cold air advection on the backside. This introduces a\nrobust pressure gradient across the Ohio Valley region on Friday.\nNorthwesterly winds will be sustained around 15 MPH, occasionally\ngusting to 30 MPH. Winds will slowly taper off Friday night into\nSaturday. \n\nThe strong cold air advection regime will drop temperatures for the\nweekend substantially compared to what we've had this week. Friday\nnight's lows fall to the upper 30s/low 40s while Saturday night we\nfall into the low/mid 30s area-wide, introducing the potential for\nfrost across the region.",
